item.description:
When building Spring Boot apps, we've found that a simple config change can make a big difference in performance. We can't stress enough how much of an impact connection pooling has on database queries. By using a connection pool like HikariCP, you can significantly reduce the time it takes to execute queries. 

For example, you can configure HikariCP in your Spring Boot app like this:
```java
@Bean
public DataSource dataSource() {
    return DataSourceBuilder.create()
            .driverClassName("org.postgresql.Driver")
            .url("jdbc:postgresql://localhost:5432/mydb")
            .username("myuser")
            .password("mypassword")
            .type(HikariDataSource.class)
            .build();
}
```
This config sets up a connection pool with the PostgreSQL driver, which can greatly improve performance. We've seen significant speedups in our own apps by making this simple change.

Defaulting to standard `@Transactional` for read-only endpoints is a silent performance killer. When you don't flag a transaction as read-only, Hibernate keeps dirty-checking your entities in memory and spends CPU cycles tracking state changes that never happen.

Adding `readOnly = true` tells Spring Boot to optimize the connection. It bypasses dirty checking, sets the transaction mode to read-only on the JDBC connection and lets PostgreSQL optimize its query plan. If you scale, you can easily route these reads to your replica databases.

Here is how you do it:

```java
@Service
public class UserService {
    private final UserRepository repository;

    @Transactional(readOnly = true)
    public UserResponse getUserProfile(Long id) {
        return repository.findById(id)
            .map(UserResponse::new)
            .orElseThrow(() -> new UserNotFoundException(id));
    }
}
```

It is a simple change that reduces CPU overhead, saves memory and prevents unexpected write locks on your database.

When working with Spring Boot and PostgreSQL, we've found that optimizing database queries can make a huge difference in performance. One simple way to do this is by using the @Query annotation in your repository interfaces. For example, if you have a User entity and you want to fetch all users with a specific role, you can use the following code:
```java
public interface UserRepository extends JpaRepository<User, Long> {
    @Query("SELECT u FROM User u WHERE u.role = :role")
    List<User> findByRole(@Param("role") String role);
}
```
This allows you to specify the exact query you want to execute, which can help reduce the amount of data being transferred and improve performance. We've seen significant improvements in our application's performance by using this approach. It's also a good idea to use pagination and sorting to limit the amount of data being fetched.

Using `GenerationType.IDENTITY` in PostgreSQL kills your batch insert performance. Hibernate doesn't batch inserts with identity because it has to execute an INSERT for every single row just to get the generated ID back. This wastes database connections, increases CPU usage and inflates your server bills.

Switch to `GenerationType.SEQUENCE`. It lets Hibernate fetch a block of IDs upfront and run a single batch insert.

```java
@Entity
public class Payment {
    @Id
    @GeneratedValue(strategy = GenerationType.SEQUENCE, generator = "pay_seq")
    @SequenceGenerator(name = "pay_seq", sequenceName = "payment_id_seq", allocationSize = 50)
    private Long id;
}
```

Now configure your application properties to enable batching:

```properties
spring.jpa.properties.hibernate.jdbc.batch_size=50
spring.jpa.properties.hibernate.order_inserts=true
```

This simple configuration change reduces your network roundtrips from 50 to 1, saving memory and keeping your database hosting costs under control.
